<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Hellenic Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1935 CE · capital Athens</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Greek republic of 1924-1935, born from the Asia Minor disaster and marked by coups, refugee resettlement and the last Venizelos government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece's</a> defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 1922 discredited the monarchy. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-revolution">military revolution</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> to abdicate, and in March 1924 the assembly proclaimed a republic, confirmed by a referendum in April. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a> was thus replaced, with Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavlos-kountouriotis">Pavlos Kountouriotis</a> as first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic had to absorb more than a million refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after the population exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, resettling many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>. Politics were unstable: General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-pangalos">Theodoros Pangalos</a> ruled as dictator in 1925-1926. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Venizelos</a> government of 1928-1932 brought stability, a peace treaty with Turkey and public works, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> ended it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Royalists won the 1933 elections, and a failed Venizelist coup in March 1935 strengthened them. In October 1935 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-kondylis">Georgios Kondylis</a> seized power, and a rigged plebiscite restored the monarchy, bringing back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-greece">George II</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-hellenic-republic.jpg" alt="Second Hellenic Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: (of code) Makaristos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece_(1822-1978).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1935 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Athens</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pavlos Kountouriotis, Theodoros Pangalos, Alexandros Zaimis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hellenic Republic (1924-1935)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=second-hellenic-republic" aria-label="Second Hellenic Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5271 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Hellenic Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5591 1068L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Athens. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=second-hellenic-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
